User Management API — Easy Notes
Approach used
I followed the project’s existing Clean Architecture. Each layer has a separate job:

Controller → Service → Repository → Database

Controller: Receives the HTTP request, calls the service, and returns the right HTTP status.
Service: Applies the user rules—validates the username, email, and role; checks for duplicates; and hashes passwords.
Repository: Reads and saves users asynchronously through Entity Framework Core.
Database: Stores the user details and enforces unique usernames and emails.
What the API does
The endpoints are in UsersController.cs:

GET /api/Users — list users
GET /api/Users/{id} — get one user
POST /api/Users — create a user
PUT /api/Users/{id} — update user details, role, and optionally password
PATCH /api/Users/{id}/status — activate or deactivate a user

Important protections
Admin-only access: The controller requires the CanManageUsers policy, which accepts only users with the Admin role.
Password safety: Passwords are hashed; API responses use a DTO that does not include the password hash.
Duplicate prevention: Usernames and emails are checked before saving, with unique database indexes as an additional safeguard.
Last active Admin: The repository checks inside a transaction that changing a user’s role or status won’t leave the system without an active Admin. The related exception is LastActiveAdminException.cs.
In simple terms: the controller handles the request, the service makes the decisions, the repository handles the database, and the safety checks protect users and the system.
